window.BIZ_PAPERS = (window.BIZ_PAPERS || []).concat([{"id": "EARL2-SAM", "unit": 2, "key": "SAM", "series": "Sample assessment (AAQ)", "date": "Pearson sample assessment material", "title": "Pearson sample paper", "source": "pearson", "kind": "exam", "code": "Sample", "spec": "new", "total": 80, "minutes": 105, "items": [{"q": 1, "sub": "a", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "Which two of these should be reported to the police when working with children\nin the nursery.\nA  Concerns about children’s welfare\nB  Spillage of dangerous chemicals\nC  Outbreaks of infectious diseases\nD  Storage of children’s data", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each of the following, up to a\nmaximum of two marks:\nA Concerns about child welfare\nE Child protection concerns", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "A", "ctx": ["E Child protection concerns"], "text": "Early years settings should include their health and safety policy on their website.\nState two aspects that should be included within the health and safety policy.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for any of the following, up to a\nmaximum of two marks:\nRIDDOR  (Infectious diseases)\nManual Handling\nCOSHH (Chemical hazards)\nFire Safety\nFood Hygiene\nManaging Allergies\nAdministering Medicines\nInjury or Accident Policy\nRisk Assessment Policy\nAccept any other appropriate policies.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "c", "marks": 1, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "The Health and Safety Act is an example of legislation.\nState one other piece of legislation relating to health and safety that settings\nshould have a policy for.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for any of the following:\n• Food Hygiene Regulations (2006)\n• Control of Hazardous Substances / COSHH\n(2006)\n• Diseases and Dangerous Occurrences\nRegulations / RIDDOR (1995)\nDo not accept Health and Safety Act.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "d", "marks": 4, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain two ways staff can ensure children will be kept safe if there is a fire in the\nearly years setting.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the identification and one\nadditional mark for the appropriate expansion to a\nmaximum of four marks.\n• Make sure detection equipment (fire/smoke\nalarms) is checked regularly/working (1) so there\nwill be early warning of fire (1)\n• Practice emergency evacuation procedures\nshould be in place (1) so staff know how to lead\nthe children to safety (1)\n• Ensure that all staff know what their role is (1)\nso that they will follow the setting’s evacuation\nprocedures (1)\nAccept any other appropriate response.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "a", "marks": 3, "section": "A", "ctx": ["Read the following scenario carefully. The questions in this section relate to this scenario. Lorton Infant School is situated on a busy road. Mrs Morden is the Year 1 class teacher. She has had full training to take children on outings. As it is going to be sunny tomorrow, she is planning to take the children to the local playground. The playground is old and is situated in the middle of the local park. The wider park area is used by dogwalkers and groups of people who meet up in the park at night to share drinks and sometimes use drugs."], "text": "State three potential hazards Mrs Morden will need to take into consideration to\nreduce risk whilst in the park.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for any of the following, up to a\nmaximum of three marks:\n• Dog mess\n• Broken bottles\n• Used needles\n• Broken equipment\n• Bad weather conditions.\nAccept any other appropriate hazards.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "b", "marks": 4, "section": "A", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain two control measures that can be put in place to ensure the children are\nsafe during the outing.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the identification and one\nadditional mark for the appropriate expansion to a\nmaximum of four marks.\n• Check weather conditions (1) to ensure\nequipment is not slippery (1)\n• Ensure there is a qualified first aider on the\nouting (1) because children may hurt themselves\n(1)\n• Use suncream if it is sunny (1) this will prevent\nsunburn (1)\n• Take protective gloves (1) to remove any glass\nwhich children may cut themselves on (1)\n• Check the play area before children enter (1) to\nensure there is no dog mess/sharps (1)\nAccept any other appropriate response.\nDo not accept responses that are unrelated to the\nouting in the case study.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "c", "marks": 9, "section": "A", "ctx": ["Mrs Morden carries out a risk assessment before the outing."], "text": "Discuss how carrying out the risk assessment will help Mrs Morden manage the\nrisks of this outing.", "ms": {"body": "Learners might refer to some/all of the following in\ntheir responses, but learners should be rewarded for\nother pertinent contextualised answers.\n• Risk assessments ensure risks are identified and\ncan be contained\n• The first stage of a risk assessment is to identify\nrisk and hazard\n• This provides a realistic assessment of the\nhazards which may be in place and the risks that\noccur because of them\n• Step two is to determine who is at risk and how\nthey may be affected, this would tell us whether\nthe children, staff or anyone else could be\nunsafe because of the identified hazards\n• Evaluation of risks and hazards which exists\nwhich provides an idea of control measures\nwhich could be put in place\n• Recording of findings is important to provide a\nwritten record and will enable findings to be\nfollowed and later reviewed\n• Monitor the risk assessment to make sure things\nhave not changed\n• Review the risk assessment to make any\nadaptations to changes\n• Risk assessment should be carried out before\nvisits and activities to give some idea of the risks\nand hazards\n• They keep children and staff safe.\nLevel\nDescriptor\nMark scheme (award up to 9 marks) refer to the guidance for how to apply levels-\nbased mark schemes*.\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1 (analysis).\n0\nNo rewardable material\n26 1\n1 - 3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the situation by\nsuperficially breaking down the different aspects into\ntheir component parts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is partially relevant to the context\nof the question and may consider only one side of\nthe context.\n2\n4 - 6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the situation by\nbreaking down the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is relevant to the context of the\nquestion and considers both sides of the context.\n7 - 9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the situation by\ncomprehensively breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of\nknowledge and understanding that is consistently\nrelevant to the context of the question and considers\nboth sides of the context in a balanced way.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "d", "marks": 12, "section": "A", "ctx": ["A balanced approach to managing risk in play involves bringing together both the risks and benefits of the activity in a single process."], "text": "Assess the risks and benefits of a balanced approach when Mrs Morden is taking\nthe children on the outing.", "ms": {"body": "Learners might refer to some/all of the following in\ntheir responses, but learners should be rewarded for\nother pertinent contextualised answers.\n• Children have rights which need to be taken into\nconsideration\n• Children’s views should be listened to and taken\ninto account\n• Provides confidence building and makes\nchildren aware of their own capabilities\n• Opportunities to take risk under supervision\n• Taking risks in controlled environments enables\nchildren to assess the risk themselves\n• Early years practitioner acts as role model for\nchildren to copy risk\n• Children learn from their mistakes\n• Child safety is paramount\n• Correct procedures must be in place\n• Provides equal opportunities in a safe\nenvironment\n• Judgements must be made to ensure safety\ncomes first\n• Risk assessment findings should be applied.\nLevel\nDescriptor\nMark scheme (award up to 12 marks) refer to the guidance for how to apply levels-\nbased mark schemes*.\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1 (analysis) and\ntrait 3 (evaluation)\n0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1 - 4\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the situation by\nsuperficially breaking down the different aspects into\ntheir component parts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is partially relevant to the context\nof the question.\n28\n• Demonstrates a basic assessment which partially\nconsiders different factors/events and their relative\nimportance, leading to a conclusion which is\nsuperficial or unsupported.\n2\n5 - 8\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the situation by\nbreaking down the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is relevant to the context of the\nquestion.\n• Demonstrates a good assessment which considers\ndifferent factors/events and their relative\nimportance, leading to a conclusion which is partially\nsupported.\n9 - 12\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the situation by\ncomprehensively breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of\nknowledge and understanding that is consistently\nrelevant to the context of the question.\n• Demonstrates a thorough assessment which\ncomprehensively considers different factors/events\nand their relative importance, leading to a conclusion\nwhich is well supported.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "a", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Which one of these helps to ensure safety for children when using the internet.\nA  Set the home page to a child-friendly website\nB  Give all children a secure password to use\nC  Allocate equal timeslots to each child\nD  Give all children full access to YouTube", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the following:\nA Set the home page to a child-friendly website", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "b", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Which one of these enables children to use technology safely?\nA  Use a shared password for all the children\nB  Allow children to use a search engine freely\nC  Only use devices belonging to the setting\nD  Encourage the use of own mobile devices", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the following:\nC Only use devices belonging to the setting", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "c", "marks": 3, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Multi agency working is very important in order to protect children."], "text": "State three statutory agencies involved at a local level in protecting children.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for any the following, up to a\nmaximum of three marks:\n• Police\n• Social services\n• Health services\n• Local Children’s Safeguarding Board", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "d", "marks": 4, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Accurate management of a child’s information is very important."], "text": "Explain two ways that an early years setting should manage a child’s personal\ninformation.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the identification and one\nadditional mark for the appropriate expansion to a\nmaximum of four marks.\n• By using passwords and/or secure place to store\nrecords (1) because personal information should\nbe kept confidential (1)\n• Information should only be shared on a need-to-\nknow basis (1) so that safeguarding procedures\nare effective (1)\n• Follow the setting’s policies on data protection\n(1) to ensure information is stored appropriately\nby everyone (1)\nAccept any other valid response.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "a", "marks": 1, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Read the following scenario carefully. The questions in this section relate to this scenario. Happy Buddies Nursery has children from the age of three up to five. Each child has a key person. Callum is the key person to Melanie aged four. Melanie has been attending the nursery since she was two years old."], "text": "State one way settings could ensure staff are recruited appropriately.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for any the following:\n• DBS checking\n• Relevant qualifications / Licence to Practice\n• Have a safer recruiting policy in place\n• Relevant experience\nAccept any other valid response.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "b", "marks": 2, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "State two ways in which settings could provide opportunities for staff to develop\nin their role.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for any the following, up to a\nmaximum of two marks:\n• Internal training\n• Guest speakers\n• Work shadowing\n• Appraisals\n• On the job qualifications\n• Supervision\n• Professional reflection\nAccept any other valid response.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "c", "marks": 4, "section": "B", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain two aspects of Callum’s role as key person to Melanie, in relation to his\nsafeguarding responsibilities.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the identification and one\nadditional mark for the appropriate expansion to a\nmaximum of four marks.\n• Offering unconditional regard for the child (1) to\nencourage open dialogue (1)\n• Encouraging parents/carers to express concerns\nor anxieties (1) in order to direct them to\nappropriate advice and guidance (1)\n• Having good relationships with other agencies\n(1) to allow causes for concern to be expressed\nin a timely way (1)\n• Having a good understanding of their own role\nin recording details of potential issues (1) in\norder to inform relevant others (1)\n• Having a good understanding of reporting\nprocedures (1) to allow timely escalation to the\nsafeguarding lead (1)\nAccept any other valid response.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "d", "marks": 6, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Melanie is normally chatty and lively but has been very quiet recently."], "text": "Explain two ways Callum can use active listening when interacting with Melanie.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for the identification and one\nadditional mark for the appropriate linked expansion\nand one mark for a further expansion of each\nidentification, up to a maximum of six marks.\n• Using verbal and non-verbal feedback (1) to\nencourage Melanie to expand (1) will enable\nCallum to gain an overall view of what Melanie is\ntelling him (1)\n• Callum can give her his full attention (1) so that\nMelanie feels at ease (1) to be able to express\nher issues/feelings clearly (1)\n• Callum can get down to Melanie’s level (1) so he\ncan make eye contact with her (1) to make her\nfeel confident to express herself (1)\nAccept any other valid response.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "e", "marks": 9, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Melanie discloses that she often gets shouted at and slapped by her mother. She also told Callum she is left on her own sometimes in the evening."], "text": "Discuss the correct procedures Callum should follow to respond to Melanie’s\ndisclosure of abuse.", "ms": {"body": "Learners might refer to some/all of the following in\ntheir responses, but learners should be rewarded for\nother pertinent contextualised answers.\n• Follow the reporting procedures of the setting\n• Know who to report to\n• Firstly report to the safeguarding lead or if not\nappropriate report to social services, NSPCC or\nPolice\n• Work within own role responsibilities\n• Work within the child’s best interest\n• Listen to the child\n• Do not discriminate\n• Accurate and timely reporting of what has been\nobserved: date, time, what has been observed,\nwhat the child has said, how you have\nresponded\n• Records should be legible, confidential, up to\ndate and should have been completed within 24\nhours of the event.\nLevel\nDescriptor\nMark scheme (award up to 9 marks) refer to the guidance for how to apply levels-\nbased mark schemes*.\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1 (analysis).\n0\nNo rewardable material\n1\n1 - 3\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the situation by\nsuperficially breaking down the different aspects into\ntheir component parts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is partially relevant to the context\nof the question and may consider only one side of\nthe context.\n2\n4 - 6\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the situation by\nbreaking down the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n34\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is relevant to the context of the\nquestion and considers both sides of the context.\n7 - 9\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the situation by\ncomprehensively breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of\nknowledge and understanding that is consistently\nrelevant to the context of the question and considers\nboth sides of the context in a balanced way.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "f", "marks": 12, "section": "B", "ctx": ["Three children who are refugees have just joined the nursery. The nursery is considering putting the children in a separate group to develop their language skills before engaging in activities with the other children."], "text": "Evaluate whether the proposed approach for the refugee children is appropriate.", "ms": {"body": "Learners might refer to some/all of the following in\ntheir responses, but learners should be rewarded for\nother pertinent contextualised answers.\n• Inclusion is understanding individuals, families,\ncultures, societies, settings and practitioners,\nand working respectfully to eliminate\ndiscriminatory and exclusionary practices\n• Separation of the refugee children\n• Keeping children in a separate room would be\nan exclusionary practice as they are excluded\nfrom other activities\n• Refugee children would not be able to integrate\nwith the other children\n• Separation from the main group may start\nexclusive practices amongst the children\n• Refugee children may only socialise with their\nown group\n• Separation could lead to discrimination\n• Separation could lead to bullying from the other\nchildren\nInclusion means listening to young children (and\ntheir families) and being sensitive to their needs\nand interests\n• Keeping refugee children in their own group\nwould enable practitioners to develop knowledge of\neach child, and their unique ways of communication\n• Listen to every child to address concerns\n• Address each child’s needs and work with them\nindividually\n• Promote a bond between the practitioner and\nthe child\nLevel\nDescriptor\nMark scheme (award up to 12 marks) refer to the guidance for how to apply levels-\nbased mark schemes*.\nThe second trait (application) carries twice as much weighting as trait 1 (analysis) and\ntrait 3 (evaluation).\n0\nNo rewardable material\n36 1\n1 - 4\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis of the situation by\nsuperficially breaking down the different aspects into\ntheir component parts.\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is partially relevant to the context\nof the question and may consider only one side of\nthe context.\n• Demonstrates a basic evaluation which partially\nconsiders different factors/events and competing\npoints, leading to a conclusion which is superficial or\nunsupported.\n2\n5 - 8\n• Demonstrates a good analysis of the situation by\nbreaking down the different aspects into their\ncomponent parts.\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is relevant to the context of the\nquestion and considers both sides of the context.\n• Demonstrates a good evaluation which considers\ndifferent factors/events and competing points,\nleading to a conclusion which is partially supported.\n9 - 12\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis of the situation by\ncomprehensively breaking down the different\naspects into their component parts.\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of\nknowledge and understanding that is consistently\nrelevant to the context of the question and considers\nboth sides of the context in a balanced way.\n• Demonstrates a thorough evaluation which\ncomprehensively considers different factors/events\nand competing points, leading to a conclusion which\nis well supported.\n37\nApril 2026\nFor information about Pearson Qualifications, including Pearson Edexcel\nand BTEC qualifications visit qualifications.pearson.com\nEdexcel and BTEC are registered trademarks of Pearson Education Limited\nPearson Education Limited. Registered in England and Wales No. 872828\nRegistered Office: 80 Strand, London WC2R 0RL.\nVAT Reg No GB 278 537121", "levels": null}}], "badge": "Pearson · current spec", "badgeTone": "new", "notice": "Pearson’s sample assessment for the current AAQ specification: the closest match to the real exam. Figures, tables and information booklets are described in words."}]);
